<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs 
<br class="ltx_break">with Counterfactual Rationale</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_doi"><span class="ltx_note_name">DOI: </span><a href="https://doi.org/XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX">XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX</a></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>ACM International Conference on Multimedia; October 27–31, 2025; Dublin, Ireland</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_isbn"><span class="ltx_note_name">ISBN: </span>978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2018/06</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Information systems Question answering</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Computing methodologies Causal reasoning and diagnostics</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiao Liang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Jiawei Hu<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Di Wang<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">1,*</sup>,
Zhi Ma<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">1,*</sup> ,
Lin Zhao<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>,
Ronghan Li<sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Bo Wan<sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Quan Wang<sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id9" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> The Key Laboratory of Smart Human-Computer Interaction 
<br class="ltx_break">and Wearable Technology of Shaanxi Province, Xidian University, Xi’an, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id10" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> Nanjing University of Science and Technology, Nanjing, China

</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_teaserfigure"><img src="2507.06959v1/Figure1_moti_v2.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/151;" width="476" height="151" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Naively adopting preference alignment for real-world clinical data makes it difficult to handle imbalanced distributions and challenging cases. Our <span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CheXPO</span> employs preference optimization through hard example mining to balance the data distribution and automatically constructs counterfactuals as rejection responses to deal with challenging cases, resulting in more reliable outputs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-language models (VLMs) are prone to hallucinations that critically compromise reliability in medical applications. While preference optimization can mitigate these hallucinations through clinical feedback, its implementation faces challenges such as clinically irrelevant training samples, imbalanced data distributions, and prohibitive expert annotation costs. To address these challenges, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CheXPO</span>, a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Che</span>st <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">X</span>-ray <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization strategy that combines confidence-similarity joint mining with counterfactual rationale. Our approach begins by synthesizing a unified, fine-grained multi-task chest X-ray visual instruction dataset across different question types for supervised fine-tuning (SFT). We then identify hard examples through token-level confidence analysis of SFT failures and use similarity-based retrieval to expand hard examples for balancing preference sample distributions, while synthetic counterfactual rationales provide fine-grained clinical preferences, eliminating the need for additional expert input. Experiments show that CheXPO achieves 8.93% relative performance gain using only 5% of SFT samples, reaching state-of-the-art performance across diverse clinical tasks and providing a scalable, interpretable solution for real-world radiology applications. The code and dataset are available here <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        <a href="https://github.com/ResearchGroup-MedVLLM/CheX-Phi35V" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ResearchGroup-MedVLLM/CheX-Phi35V</a></span></span></span>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Medical Vision-Language Model, Direct Preference Optimization
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding authors.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have driven advances in open-domain dialogue and understanding across various domains. In the medical field, numerous approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moor et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Omkar Thawkar and Khan, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rongsheng Wang and Tan, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have emerged, aimed at tasks such as image interpretation, pathology analysis, and medical question answering. These methods build large-scale medical visual instruction datasets and employ supervised fine-tuning (SFT) to adapt general-purpose VLMs to medical tasks. However, these medical vision-language models still face reliability issues, particularly factual errors, also known as “<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hallucinations</span>,” where models generate responses that appear coherent but contradict the image information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hein et al., 2024</a>)</cite> attributes the primary cause of hallucinations in MedVLMs to modality misalignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and attempts to improve alignment through constructing preference data to optimize models. However, the preference data generation processes for these methods largely follow approaches from the general text-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morimura et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or vision-language domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, overlooking the unique characteristics and requirements of real clinical scenarios. Specifically, the following challenges exist: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">① Training samples lack clinical significance.</span> Questions such as “<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is this image an AP view or a PA view?</span>” can be easily addressed by an SFT model and offer limited value for clinical diagnostics, diminishing their usefulness for preference alignment. <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">② Long-tailed distribution in real clinical data</span>. Medical datasets often exhibit imbalances between common diseases (e.g., “<span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pleural Effusion</span>”) and rare diseases (e.g., “<span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chylothorax</span>”), which leads to biased model outputs that favor more common diseases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, undermining the model’s ability to accurately diagnose rare diseases. <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">③ High cost of preference data creation</span>. Generating preference data for medical image responses requires expert evaluation, making the process costly and limiting scalability, which hinders further improvements in model performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CheXPO</span>, a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization strategy for <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Che</span>st <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">X</span>-ray vision-language models, designed to mitigate the aforementioned issues through confidence-similarity joint hard example mining and counterfactual rationale, as shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Specifically, we unify multiple benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> into a large-scale visual instruction dataset containing 3 specialized question-answering tasks (Basic-QA, Region-QA, and Comparison-QA), 10 question types, and 640k expert-level rationales, enabling general VLMs to interpret chest X-rays through SFT. The proposed CheXPO enhances the reliability of SFT models through three steps: <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">①</span> Token-level confidence analysis on SFT models identifies clinically significant responses, including errors and low-confidence responses as hard examples. <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">②</span> Similarity-based hard example expansion with BioMedCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, retrieving semantically similar samples to balance the long-tailed distributions of the preference dataset. <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">③</span> Constructing counterfactual rationale as rejection responses, leveraging preference optimization to reinforce fine-grained supervision, particularly for misjudgment errors related to abnormality, anatomy, and severity. Our proposed CheXPO demonstrates strong scalability, eliminating the need for expert annotations or GPT API dependencies, while maintaining cost-effectiveness and implementation efficiency for large-scale clinical applications. Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A large-scale synthetic chest X-ray vision-language instruction dataset covering basic, region, and comparison tasks, with 10 clinically fine-grained question types and 640k expert-level rationales for supervised fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A scalable preference optimization strategy, CheXPO, utilizes data from only 5% of the SFT samples through confidence-similarity-joint hard example mining and counterfactual rationale, achieving an 8.93% relative improvement in performance without additional expert annotations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CheX-Phi3.5V</span>, outperforms existing methods on the MIMIC-CXR-VQA and Medical-Diff-VQA benchmarks, demonstrating improved interpretability and reliability, thereby better meeting practical clinical needs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Medical Vision-Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The development of vision-language models (VLMs) has significantly advanced task-specific applications in the medical field. Using GPT-based instruction generation, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) has become the primary method for adapting general-purpose VLMs to meet medical needs. LLaVA-Med <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> employs alignment and instruction tuning to enable open-ended question answering for biomedical images. Med-Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moor et al., 2023</a>)</cite> enhances few-shot reasoning through a multimodal context learning framework. RadFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> integrates large-scale radiology datasets for more robust multi-task image analysis. XrayGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Omkar Thawkar and Khan, 2023</a>)</cite> and XrayGLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rongsheng Wang and Tan, 2023</a>)</cite> focus on multi-turn QA and automated chest X-ray interpretation. HuatuoVision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> generates 1.3 million VQA instances to train high-quality question-answering models. BioMed-VITAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>)</cite> integrates expert-selected data, which notably enhances its performance in complex clinical scenarios. As VLMs continue to advance, scaling synthetic SFT data broadens their scenario coverage and enhances MedVLM robustness. However, in real-world clinical settings, the quality of medical datasets—such as long-tail distributions, unverifiable missing data, or erroneous entries—poses significant challenges to the reliability of MedVLMs. Moreover, SFT’s focus on next-token prediction—a task inherently biased toward memorizing training patterns—limits its ability to generalize, thereby constraining adaptability to the complex and evolving demands of clinical practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Human Feedback in VLM Alignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome SFT limitations and improve model reliability, reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) has gained prominence. Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> offers a simpler, cost-effective alternative, widely used to enhance model alignment with preferences. DPO relies heavily on well-chosen preference pairs, which, if noisy, risk suboptimal policies or overfitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Recent approaches address these challenges through improved data selection and filtering. Selective DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> employs margin-based filtering for higher-quality pairs, while Filtered DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morimura et al., 2024</a>)</cite> excludes unreliable samples. MIA-DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses attention mechanisms to select rejected samples and reduce noise. CLIP-DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouali et al., 2024</a>)</cite> filters mismatched pairs via CLIP, enhancing preference data reliability. In medical applications, STLLaVA-Med <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> combines self-supervised generation and DPO fine-tuning to align with radiologist preferences. CheXalign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hein et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses reference-based alignment, while RULE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and MMed-RAG employ retrieval-based strategies to improve preference selection. MMedPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> applies multimodal optimization, reducing hallucinations and improving diagnostic accuracy. While existing methods enhance VLM factual consistency and hallucination mitigation through modality/data-source adaptations, they inadequately address practical chest X-ray diagnostic challenges (e.g., fine-grained pathology analysis) and suffer from low data efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminary: Dataset Synthesis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To build a comprehensive chest X-ray visual instruction dataset, we combine four sources: MIMIC-CXR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al., 2019</a>)</cite> for images and reports; Chest ImaGenome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> for expert-labeled organs and diseases; and MIMIC-CXR-VQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Medical-Diff-VQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for all image QA pairs. Despite the extensive clinical questions provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, their short answers are insufficient to equip vision-language models with dialogue and rationale generation capabilities. Given that these datasets share chest X-rays, study IDs, and subject IDs, MIMIC-CXR provides radiologist reports, supporting fine-grained medical reasoning and structured diagnostic interpretations. Therefore, we leverage the powerful GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>, synthesizing existing questions/answers with novel GPT-4o-generated rationales and clinical attributes to simulate expert diagnostic processes, significantly reducing manual annotation costs. Below is a detailed description of each task:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic-QA</span>. We utilize a structured prompting template that integrates existing question-answer pairs from MIMIC-CXR-VQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and corresponding radiology reports from MIMIC-CXR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. The model is instructed to reference these reports and infer supporting evidence from the image to justify the given answer. It should be emphasized that the rationale <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> synthesized by GPT-4o must begin with a short answer <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. The short answer can either be a single word, such as "<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Yes</span>," or a phrase composed of multiple words, such as "<span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left lung and right lung</span>." The explanation <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> must be provided from the perspective of an expert reviewing the image, answering the question <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and supporting the gold standard answers provided in MIMIC-CXR-VQA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Region-QA</span>. To enhance interpretability, we build on existing question-answer pairs by adding explicit visual prompts via alpha-channel markers, and we replace specific anatomical terms in the question with placeholders such as <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"marked region"</span> or <span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"highlighted area."</span> The visual prompts refer to the bounding box coordinates provided by the Chest ImaGenome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> silver dataset. To further enhance spatial flexibility, we additionally incorporate prompt types such as arrows, ellipses, or asterisks (*), following the approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The arrowheads are positioned within a constrained region <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[(-w/2,-h/2)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(-w/2,-h/2),</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(w/2,h/2)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w/2,h/2)]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> represent the bounding box width and height, respectively. For ellipses, the axes inherit dimensions from the bounding box and are scaled within a range of <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,1.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,1.5]</annotation></semantics></math>. These augmentations are inspired by visual prompts commonly seen in PubMed figures, where experts directly overlay markers on images for clearer illustration, providing a more intuitive and flexible approach compared to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison-QA</span>. Medical-Diff-VQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> provides study IDs for both the main and reference chest X-rays being compared. We leverage these IDs to retrieve their respective radiology reports, using them as contrastive evidence. GPT-4o is then prompted to generate detailed comparative explanations based on the main report and the prior report. Similar to Basic and Region-QA, we instruct GPT-4o to emulate expert image analysis, requiring synthesized rationales to derive exclusively from visual evidence while strictly avoiding references to textual reports or prior diagnostic annotations. We unify the question types from MIMIC-Ext-VQA and Med-Diff-VQA into 10 clinically relevant categories, including Abnormality, Presence, Location, View, Level, Type, Difference, Size, Gender, and Attribute.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3. Preliminary: Dataset Synthesis ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates examples of the three tasks. To ensure fair benchmarking, we follow the original train/validation/test splits, with additional cleaning to address missing MIMIC-CXR reports, absent Chest ImaGenome bounding boxes, and overlapping images across benchmarks. Detailed descriptions of each data source and final dataset statistics, along with detailed counts and rationale generation prompts, can be found in the Appendix. All the above datasets are publicly available on the PhysioNet platform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">PhysioNet, [n. d.]</a>)</cite> and require credentialed access approval.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06959v1/Figure2_example.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/329;" width="476" height="329" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>
<span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example of integrated multi-task QA dataset.</span> Each rationale <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> includes a <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-bg-color:#B266FF;">short answer <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-bg-color:#B266FF;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#B266FF" mathcolor="#FFFFFF">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and a GPT-4o-generated <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBDBDB;">explanation <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DBDBDB;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathbackground="#DBDBDB">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-bg-color:#00CC00;">"annotation area"</span> indicates the visual prompt on the image.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06959v1/Figure3_overview.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/153;" width="429" height="153" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of our chest X-ray VLM training pipeline.</span>
Our framework leverages visual instruction tuning and preference alignment, fine-tuning Phi-3.5V with LoRA to obtain a base SFT model.
A confidence-similarity joint mining strategy selects high-value samples, followed by counterfactual rationale construction for preference alignment to reinforce clinical reliability.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce our training pipeline, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3. Preliminary: Dataset Synthesis ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We first fine-tune Phi-3.5V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> with the synthetic instruction dataset to obtain a supervised base model, CheX-Phi3.5V. The <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CheXPO</span> strategy is then employed, utilizing confidence-similarity joint mining to select hard examples, followed by counterfactual rationale construction to obtain preference pairs for preference alignment. The complete method workflow is detailed in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4.3. Counterfactual Rationale Construction ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The following sections describe each component in detail. To ensure reproducibility, we provide implementation and hyperparameter details in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Hard Example Identification</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the training pipeline of our CheX-Phi3.5V, SFT primarily teaches the model to understand medical images and follow instructions to generate answers in the desired format, while direct preference optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> further refines the model’s clinical reliability and response preference. However, imbalanced data is common in chest X-ray datasets, which tend to overrepresent healthy cases or frequent findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This imbalance can lead the SFT model to overfit to common diagnostic patterns, while DPO risks exacerbating distributional shifts by disproportionately reinforcing majority-class patterns through biased preference pairs. To address this issue, we introduce a hard example mining strategy that identifies high-value preference data for DPO training, specifically targeting cases where the base SFT model fails. Given a chest X-ray vision-language model <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(.)</annotation></semantics></math> trained via supervised fine-tuning, and an SFT training dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of images <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, questions <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and rationales <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a concise answer followed by a detailed explanation, formally represented as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=[\mathcal{A};\mathcal{E}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=[\mathcal{A};\mathcal{E}]</annotation></semantics></math>. To efficiently evaluate model performance and identify hard examples, we perform stratified random sampling from the training dataset based on question and answer types and obtain a representative subset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{sample}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{sample}</annotation></semantics></math> for initial assessment. The stratified sampling ratio, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, represents the proportion of the dataset selected, with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total training data, ensuring comprehensive coverage of all question types. Within this subset, for each image <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding question <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a token sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]</annotation></semantics></math> through auto-regressive factorization. As only the short answer segments <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> have well-defined ground truth labels, we focus on them and directly compute their log-probabilities. To prevent longer answers from being unfairly penalized due to their length, we apply a length-normalization technique inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malinin and Gales, 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=\log\tilde{\mathbf{P}}(\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}|\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q};\theta)=\frac{1}{T_{\mathcal{A}}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{\mathcal{A}}}\log\mathbf{P}(y_{t}|y_{&lt;t},\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q};\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=\log\tilde{\mathbf{P}}(\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}|\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q};\theta)=\frac{1}{T_{\mathcal{A}}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{\mathcal{A}}}\log\mathbf{P}(y_{t}|y_{&lt;t},\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q};\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{&lt;t}\triangleq[y_{1},...,y_{t-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{&lt;t}\triangleq[y_{1},...,y_{t-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the prefix sequence, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the length of the short answer sequence. When <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the predicted answer <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is directly labeled as a rejected response <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and included in a new subset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math> for DPO training, reinforcing factual grounding and reducing confident errors. Conversely, when <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is low (i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}&lt;\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}&lt;\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, a predefined threshold) yet <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is likely uncertain about its prediction. In this case, we construct contrastive responses as the rejected <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> based on expert knowledge, enhancing model differentiation through DPO training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Confidence-Similarity Joint Mining</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After initializing the hard example set <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of images <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, questions <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and rationales <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, formally represented as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathcal{V}^{(i)},\mathcal{Q}^{(i)},\mathcal{T}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathcal{V}^{(i)},\mathcal{Q}^{(i)},\mathcal{T}^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we retrieve semantically similar samples from the remaining data <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{rest}=\mathcal{D}-\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{rest}=\mathcal{D}-\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math> to further enrich the dataset for DPO training, thereby mitigating SFT failures caused by the long-tailed distribution. We first leverage BioMedCLIP  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to extract features <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the images, questions, and rationale in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we compute cosine similarity matrices <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{rest}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{rest}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, to identify samples with similar semantics and visual context:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{X}}=\Big[\langle f_{\mathcal{X}}^{(i)},f_{\mathcal{X}}^{(j)}\rangle\Big]_{\begin{subarray}{c}i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard}\\
j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}\end{subarray}},\quad\mathcal{X}\in\{\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{V}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{X}}=\Big[\langle f_{\mathcal{X}}^{(i)},f_{\mathcal{X}}^{(j)}\rangle\Big]_{\begin{subarray}{c}i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard}\\
j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}\end{subarray}},\quad\mathcal{X}\in\{\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{V}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> denotes cosine similarity. Final similarity combines all modalities:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{Q}}+\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{T}}+\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{V}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{Q}}+\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{T}}+\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{V}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{\mathbf{s}^{(i,j)}\}_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard},j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{\mathbf{s}^{(i,j)}\}_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard},j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then select the Top-<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> most similar samples <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th hard example based on <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{(i)}=\text{TopK}_{j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}\,\mathcal{S}^{(i,j)},\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>TopK</mtext><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{(i)}=\text{TopK}_{j\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}\,\mathcal{S}^{(i,j)},\quad\forall i\in\mathcal{D}_{hard},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and this combined similarity ensures a balance between semantic alignment and visual consistency, as further validated in the ablation study (Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Ablation Study ‣ 6. Experimental Results ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Counterfactual Rationale Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While incorrect samples are directly used as rejected responses, correct predictions (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>) are underutilized due to unverifiable explanatory rationales <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, limiting data efficiency. Given that low-confidence predictions are prone to errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahaut et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we analyze samples with log-probability scores <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> below the threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and find that <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Abnormality</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Anatomy</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Severity</span> misjudgments collectively account for over 70% of model errors (see Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Ablation Study ‣ 6. Experimental Results ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>). In response, we propose a chest X-ray-specific counterfactual rationale construction strategy by designing domain-specific rejection pools:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anatomy Rejection Pool:</span> Contains semantically similar but clinically opposite anatomical terms (e.g., “<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">left lung opacity</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">right lung opacity</span>”) to generate counterfactual rationale.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abnormality Rejection Pool:</span> Includes confusable disease conditions (e.g., “<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pneumonia</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pulmonary edema, atelectasis</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pneumothorax</span>”) to contrast common misdiagnoses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Severity Rejection Pool:</span> Modifies severity indicators (e.g., “<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mild interstitial infiltrates</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">severe consolidation</span>”, “<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">acute phase</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chronic stage</span>”) to introduce graded diagnostic contradictions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, for MedVLM-generated <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, we first classify <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s type and randomly replace it using the corresponding rejection pool, obtaining <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. For question types such as <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gender</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">View</span>, where answers have clear opposites (e.g., “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">female</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">male</span>”, “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AP view</span>” vs. “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PA view</span>”), we directly use the opposite answer as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For types like <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Size</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Type</span>, we randomly sample a different answer of the same type as a contrastive replacement. We then retrieve the Top-1 contrastive candidate <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on BioMedCLIP:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}}=\underset{\mathcal{T}_{k}\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}{\arg\max}\ \langle f_{\mathcal{T}_{k}},f_{[\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒯</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}}=\underset{\mathcal{T}_{k}\in\mathcal{D}_{rest}}{\arg\max}\ \langle f_{\mathcal{T}_{k}},f_{[\overline{\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we assign <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, serving as the rejected response in the preference pair used for alignment. This ensures rejected responses maintain semantic coherence while presenting clinically counterfactual rationale.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>CheXPO Strategy Pipeline</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>
Dataset <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T}=[\mathcal{A};\mathcal{E}])\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>;</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T}=[\mathcal{A};\mathcal{E}])\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>; 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline">Sampled subset <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{sampled}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{sampled}</annotation></semantics></math>;
Med-LVLM <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Preference alignment dataset <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Initialize <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})\in\mathcal{D}_{sampled}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})\in\mathcal{D}_{sampled}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}\leftarrow\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}\leftarrow\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            Add <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathbf{y}_{w},\mathbf{y}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathbf{y}_{w},\mathbf{y}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.10.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})\in\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})\in\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            Define <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{rest}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{rest}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
            Retrieve TopK similar samples <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{rest}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{rest}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.2. Confidence-Similarity Joint Mining ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.13.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=[\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}};\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{E}}]\leftarrow\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12</span></span>
            Compute length-normalized log-prob <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1. Hard Example Identification ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.16" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.16.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}\neq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.17" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">14</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}\leftarrow\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}\leftarrow\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.18" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">15</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else if</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.18.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}\land\mathbf{p}&lt;\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{\mathcal{A}}=\mathcal{A}\land\mathbf{p}&lt;\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.19" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{w}\leftarrow\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, generate <math id="algorithm1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.3. Counterfactual Rationale Construction ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>);
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.20" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">17</span></span>
            Add <math id="algorithm1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathbf{y}_{w},\mathbf{y}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Q},\mathbf{y}_{w},\mathbf{y}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{hard}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{hard}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.21" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
