<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Data Sampling and Size Considerations</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss the sampling parameters and sample proportions for different preference-aligned data used in the experiments, as shown in Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix D Comparison with Existing Benchmarks ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represents the uniform random sampling ratio from the original training set <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, while TopK refers to the multi-modal retrieval described in Equation (4). The <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the threshold for the log-prob <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation (1), where only the correct samples with a log-prob <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> lower than <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are used to generate contrastive rejection <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. The "<span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">#SFT Fails</span>" column represents the use of incorrect predictions from the SFT model as <math id="A5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, while "<span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">#CRej</span>" indicates the use of the method described in Subsection 4.3 for generating <math id="A5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Data samples ranging from 1k to 20k were used in ablation experiments, and the 30k sample size achieved the best results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Data Construction and Preprocessing</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The data construction process is divided into two steps. The first step involves generating rationales for different subtasks: <span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic-QA</span>, <span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Region-QA</span>, and <span id="A6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compare-QA</span>. For the Basic-QA and Region-QA subtasks, rationales are generated using GPT-4o by extracting relevant information from the report to answer the question and provide a short answer, followed by an explanation. The rationale is constructed from the perspective of a doctor examining the images, with the short answer leading the explanation. For the Compare-QA subtask, two images are used: the main image corresponds to the current query, while the reference image is used for comparison. Rationales for this task are extracted from both reports.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The second step involves generating region-based prompts. Using a template containing the placeholder <span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">${object}</span>, the bounding box of the relevant object is located in the Chest Imagenome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> dataset by matching the corresponding <span id="A6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">study_id</span>. After locating the bounding box, the region of interest (ROI) and relevant words like “<span id="A6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">specified region</span>” are used to modify the prompt. Additionally, visual prompts are randomly added to the bbox corresponding to the image. The prompts for the basic/region and comparison subtasks are constructed as shown in Figure <a href="#A7.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix G Preference Optimisation Ablations ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A7.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix G Preference Optimisation Ablations ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, and the attributes included in each data sample are shown in Figure <a href="#A7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix G Preference Optimisation Ablations ‣ CheXPO: Preference Optimization for Chest X-ray VLMs with Counterfactual Rationale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we emphasize that all rationales in CheXPO are not generated by GPT-4 alone; rather, they are strictly derived from the original radiology reports written by clinical experts in the MIMIC-CXR dataset. To further validate their reliability, we uniformly sampled 1,000 generated counterfactual rationales. These underwent automated assessment, including Disease Correctness (measuring entity overlap with reports), Semantic Similarity (assessing rephrased information relevance), and LLM Evaluation (factual correctness scoring by GPT-4o). A subset of 100 rationales was additionally subjected to human evaluation by medical professionals for factual correctness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T9" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A6.T9.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.T9.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T9.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.45pt;padding-bottom:0.45pt;">
<span id="A6.T9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:79.3pt;">
